  <x_1999:xhtmlp>
    <x_1999:xhtmla/>
  </x_1999:xhtmlp>
  <x_1999:xhtmlh3 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Property: foaf:interest</x_1999:xhtmlh3>
  <x_1999:xhtmlem rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">interest</x_1999:xhtmlem>
  <x_1999:xhtmlbr rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"></x_1999:xhtmlbr>
  <x_1999:xhtmlp rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">
This approach to characterising interests is intended to compliment other mechanisms (such 
as the use of controlled vocabulary). It allows us to use a widely known set of unique 
identifiers (Web page URIs) with minimal pre-coordination. Since URIs have a controlled 
syntax, this makes data merging much easier than the use of free-text characterisations of 
interest.
</x_1999:xhtmlp>
